PART 6 — PRODUCT CATALOG, FIRESTORE AND SYNCHRONIZATION

Audit the complete product lifecycle.

The catalog should combine default products with custom products stored in Firestore without accidentally replacing either group.

Inspect the existing implementation of:

getProductsCatalog().

Product merging.

Stable product IDs.

Timestamp handling.

Sorting.

Firestore onSnapshot.

Catalog update callbacks.

Admin publish actions.

Product update actions.

Product deletion.

Product activation and deactivation.

Homepage showcase prioritization.

Test the following scenarios:

Scenario 1: New product

Create a test product named E2E TEST POLAROID.

Verify that it is saved, appears in the catalog, and appears in the correct section.

Scenario 2: Catalog persistence

Refresh the storefront and revisit the page.

Verify that the product still exists and that default products have not disappeared.

Scenario 3: Sorting

Verify that recently created products are ordered according to the intended sorting rule.

Check timestamp formats, missing timestamps, and ties.

Do not assume that every new product must appear first in every section. Featured products, bundles, and other curated sections may have their own legitimate sorting rules.

Scenario 4: Editing

Change the test product's price, title, and tags.

Verify that the updated data appears wherever the product is displayed.

Scenario 5: Deletion

Delete or deactivate the test product through the supported interface.

Verify that it disappears from the appropriate storefront sections.

Scenario 6: Realtime updates

Open the storefront and admin dashboard in separate sessions.

Make a change in the admin session and verify whether the storefront updates without a manual refresh.

Scenario 7: Duplicate prevention

Check whether repeated synchronization, page refreshes, or repeated publish actions produce duplicate products.

Scenario 8: Network and permission failures

Test recoverable failures where safe.

Verify that the UI displays useful error messages rather than silently losing changes.

Do not delete real products or modify customer orders during testing.

Clean up test records when appropriate and authorized.

